WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.180
In this video, you enable the harvester-seeder add-on and give a host its BMC address and credentials, so that SUSE Virtualization can manage the hardware out of band.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.940
Under Advanced, Add-ons, open the action menu of harvester-seeder.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:23.710
Select Enable. Seeder talks to the baseboard management controller of each host, over IPMI and Redfish.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.040
The add-on reports Deploy Successful.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.350
On the Hosts page, open the action menu of r640-1,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.950
and select Edit Config.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.280
Open the Out-of-band Access tab. The tab appears once the add-on is enabled.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.720
Select Enabled.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.780
Enter the address of the host's BMC, here a Dell iDRAC nine.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.450
Enter port six two three, the IPMI over LAN port.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:56.800
Select Insecure TLS, because this iDRAC uses a self-signed certificate.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.740
The credentials live in a secret. Open the Secret list and choose Create a New.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.780
Name the secret demo-idrac-r640. It is created in the default namespace.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.810
Enter the user name of a BMC account that may control power,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.480
and its password.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.750
Save the secret.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.880
Event is Enabled, so Seeder polls Redfish for hardware events every hour.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.660
Select Save.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.440
The host details now show the manufacturer, serial number, and model that Seeder read from the BMC over Redfish.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.520
The Out-of-band Access tab shows the saved BMC connection.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.060
Open the Events tab.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:47.510
It lists the hardware events that Seeder reads from Redfish, such as temperature health and failed components. Events are kept for one hour.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.420
Open the action menu of the host again.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:58.040
Shut Down, Power On, and Reboot over IPMI appear here once the host is in maintenance mode. This single-node cluster stays running.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:13.421
The host is connected to its BMC. SUSE Virtualization now shows hardware details and events, and offers out-of-band power actions for hosts in maintenance mode.
